10:22:46 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: A species is considered **extinct** when there is no reasonable doubt that its last individual has died. In practice, this is not something that can usually be proved with absolute certainty—especially for small, rare, cryptic, or poorly studied organisms such as insects. The standard is therefore not “we looked once and did not find it,” but rather: **despite adequate, targeted searches in the places, seasons, habitats, and life stages where it should be detectable, no individuals have been found, and the available evidence indicates it is no longer present.** ## How extinction is assessed Conservation organizations such as the IUCN use several kinds of evidence: - **Last confirmed records.** When and where was the species last reliably collected, photographed, or observed? - **Survey effort.** Have researchers searched the species’ known range thoroughly and repeatedly? - **Appropriate methods.** Different organisms require different methods: light traps, pitfall traps, host-plant searches, soil or leaf-litter sampling, larval surveys, acoustic recording, camera traps, and so on. - **Timing.** Searches need to occur during the correct season, time of day, weather conditions, and phase of the life cycle. An insect may only be adult for a few days or may spend years underground as a larva. - **Habitat status.** If all known habitat has been destroyed, flooded, converted to agriculture, or otherwise made unsuitable, extinction becomes much more likely. - **Biology and detectability.** A large, conspicuous bird is easier to survey than a tiny beetle that lives inside rotting wood. Scientists account for the chance of missing an organism even when it is present. - **Population trends and threats.** Long-term decline, very small known populations, invasive predators, disease, climate change, or loss of a required host species can support the conclusion that extinction is likely. Under IUCN terminology, a taxon is listed as **Extinct (EX)** when “there is no reasonable doubt that the last individual has died.” The IUCN specifically says this conclusion should be based on exhaustive surveys of known and expected habitat, conducted at appropriate times over the species’ historical range. ## Can we truly verify that none remain? Usually, no: one cannot literally search every square meter of Earth, every cave, every tree cavity, or every handful of soil. Extinction is therefore a scientific conclusion based on probability and evidence, not an absolute logical proof. This uncertainty is especially important for insects and other invertebrates because they may be: - very small or hard to identify; - active only briefly or at night; - dormant for long periods; - restricted to inaccessible habitat; - mistaken for similar species; - poorly recorded even when still common; - represented by eggs, larvae, or pupae that do not resemble the adult. For those reasons, many organisms are classified as **Critically Endangered**, **Possibly Extinct**, or **Data Deficient** rather than formally extinct when uncertainty remains. ## How scientists improve confidence Researchers use repeated, systematic surveys and statistical methods called **detection models** or **occupancy models**. These estimate the probability that a survey would detect a species if it were actually present. For example, if a beetle has a 60% chance of being found on any properly conducted survey, repeated unsuccessful surveys make continued survival increasingly unlikely. New tools can help, though they are not definitive by themselves: - **Environmental DNA (eDNA):** detecting genetic traces in water, soil, or other samples; - **DNA barcoding and museum specimens:** confirming whether old and new records really belong to the same species; - **Automated acoustic or image monitoring;** - **Remote sensing:** determining whether suitable habitat remains; - **Reports from local residents, naturalists, and Indigenous communities.** A “rediscovery” after decades of no records does happen. Such species are sometimes called **Lazarus species**. This is why responsible assessors are cautious: failure to find a species is not automatically proof that it is gone. So, extinction determinations are best understood as a high-confidence judgment: after extensive, appropriate searching and consideration of the species’ ecology and habitat, survival has become so unlikely that the species is regarded as extinct—while recognizing that absolute certainty is rarely possible.
