10:23:11 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Yes—that is a fair use of “verify.” For practical scientific purposes, we can verify that the dodo is extinct: the evidence is overwhelming, consistent, and no plausible route to continued survival remains. The earlier distinction was not meant to deny that. It was only to explain why extinction assessments use language such as “no reasonable doubt” rather than claiming a literal mathematical proof of absence. For the dodo, the evidence meets any ordinary standard of verification: - it was restricted to Mauritius; - it was large, flightless, and conspicuous; - it disappeared shortly after human settlement and introduced animals arrived; - its habitat and island have been extensively occupied and observed for centuries; - there have been no credible records or physical signs for roughly 300 years. For tiny insects, verification can be much harder because the evidence is often weaker—not because extinction cannot in principle be verified, but because the chance of overlooking surviving individuals may remain substantial.
